// Lesson 3.2 — Projectiles: Instantiate, Fire, Destroy
// SOLO BUILD (scaffold level 4). Everything a projectile needs has already been
// done once, somewhere else, wearing different clothes. So nothing here is
// given — the student splits the problem, looks each part up, and writes the
// script.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (read from the teacher's project) — and the reason this page was
// rewritten:
//   Assets/Scripts/Projectile.cs — TARGET-SEEDED, not direction-committed:
//       float speed = 8;
//       public Transform target;
//       Vector3 targetPosition;
//       [SerializeField] float lifespan = 3f;
//       Start(): if (target != null) targetPosition = target.position;
//                else targetPosition = transform.position;
//       Update(): transform.position = Vector3.MoveTowards(transform.position, targetPosition, speed * Time.deltaTime);
//                 lifespan -= Time.deltaTime; if (lifespan < 0) Destroy(gameObject);
//   Assets/Scripts/TurretController.cs — Shoot():
//       GameObject fireballCopy = Instantiate(fireballPrefab, spawnPoint.position, Quaternion.identity);
//       Projectile projectileScript = fireballCopy.GetComponent<Projectile>();
//       projectileScript.target = target;      // target is the PLAYER's Transform, set in 3.1's trigger
//   Assets/Prefabs/Turret_Projectile.prefab — tag E_Fireball; the finished
//       project gives it a CircleCollider2D set Is Trigger (that pair is 3.4's
//       work); it has NO Rigidbody2D and is moved by writing the Transform.
//
// THE OLD PAGE TAUGHT THE OPPOSITE MECHANISM: derive a direction with vector
// subtraction, normalize it, store it on the clone, and move along it. Nothing
// in the build does that. Vector3.MoveTowards() computes the direction from two
// positions every frame, so the bullet needs one thing from the turret — a
// destination — and the whole "normalized direction" part of the old lesson
// disappears. The new centre is the SNAPSHOT: the bullet reads the player's
// position once, in Start(), which is what makes it commit to the shot instead
// of homing.
//
// The parts below are NOT rendered at level 4 (see src/lib/decompose.ts) — they
// are the record of the intended split. Do not delete them as "unused".
//
// Answer-box ids are positional and persisted: the socratic block keeps its
// three questions in order (reworded, never moved) and the decompose block keeps
// its single turn (dc1:q2).
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'A turret that shoots at me. That is the whole feature, and it is far too big to hold ' +
    'in your head at once — which is exactly why every lesson in this course starts the same way.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Notice the player is close enough, and keep hold of them',
      does: 'The trigger circle from 3.1 already sets `inRange`; it also stores the player\'s `Transform` in a field called `target`. That reference is the only thing the turret needs to hand on.',
      doc: '3.1 — The Turret: the trigger radius, `inRange`, and the `target` field it writes on the way in.',
      href: '3.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Stamp out a copy of the bullet at the muzzle',
      does: '`Instantiate(fireballPrefab, spawnPoint.position, Quaternion.identity)` — clone the prefab at the SpawnPoint child, not at the turret\'s centre.',
      doc: '2.4 — Prefabs: `Instantiate(Object original, Vector3 position, Quaternion rotation)`.',
      href: '2.4.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Hand the copy a target, not a direction',
      does: '`GetComponent<Projectile>()` on the clone, then `projectileScript.target = target;` — one reference, passed across the file boundary. The turret does not compute where to aim.',
      doc: '2.1 — a component looked up with `GetComponent<T>()`; 3.1 — the `target` it stores.',
      href: '2.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Read the destination once, then walk toward it',
      does: '`targetPosition = target.position` in `Start()`, and `Vector3.MoveTowards(transform.position, targetPosition, speed * Time.deltaTime)` in `Update()`. The snapshot is the commitment; `MoveTowards()` works out the direction.',
      doc: 'Look up `Vector3.MoveTowards()` in the quick reference: it takes a start, a destination and a step, and computes the direction itself.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Give it a lifespan, then destroy it',
      does: '`lifespan -= Time.deltaTime; if (lifespan < 0) { Destroy(gameObject); }` — a countdown from 3 seconds, so misses do not pile up. The same shape becomes 3.3\'s subject next class.',
      doc: '2.1 — `Destroy(Object obj)`, the call that made a coin vanish.',
      href: '2.1.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Notice what is NOT on that list: no new API except the movement call, and even that is one ' +
    'lookup. Five parts, and the turret only supplies a position and a reference. That is what a ' +
    'well-sequenced course feels like from the inside — not "here is a new thing you have never ' +
    'seen", but "here are the same parts, handed across a file boundary".',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one ' +
    'search? "Make the turret shoot" cannot be looked up. "Clone a prefab at a position" can. ' +
    'If a part is still too big to search for, it is two parts.',
  turns: [
    'The bullet is handed a live `Transform` but reads only its position, and only once. Say which line makes that a snapshot, and what the player would see if the read happened every frame instead.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>3.2 — Projectiles: Instantiate, Fire, Destroy</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your turret knows when you are near; now it needs teeth. Everything a projectile needs, you
    have already written once, somewhere else — and the turret will hand the bullet one thing it
    cannot work out for itself. No API table this time: two files, and one lookup.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('in unity how to shoot a projectile, how to instantiate a bullet in the direction the player faces, how to destroy an object after a few seconds', 4)}

${renderDecompose('3.2', DECOMPOSE, 4)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret_Projectile</code>, then
            <strong>Inspector →</strong> an empty <strong>Projectile</strong> component — already
            attached to the prefab, waiting for the script you are about to give it. In the finished
            project this prefab also carries a <strong>Circle Collider 2D</strong> with
            <strong>Is Trigger</strong> ticked and the <code>E_Fireball</code> tag; that pair is
            <a href="3.4.html">3.4</a>'s work, not today's. Today's script needs neither, and it
            does not need a <strong>Rigidbody 2D</strong> either.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> a <strong>Turret</strong> instance in the
            <strong>Overworld</strong>, then <strong>Inspector → Turret Controller</strong>: two
            slots sit empty, and they fill up in step 3. Expand the turret and click the child
            <strong>SpawnPoint</strong> — that is the muzzle, not the turret's centre and not the
            world origin.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Once the code half is done — Inspector → Turret Controller →</strong> drag
            <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret_Projectile</code> from the <strong>Project window</strong>
            into the Fireball Prefab slot, and the <strong>Hierarchy's</strong> SpawnPoint into the
            Spawn Point slot. Do both turrets. An empty slot says nothing at startup and throws the
            instant you step into range, which looks like a firing bug and is a setup bug.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view → press Play</strong> and walk into range: a projectile appears at the
            muzzle and travels. Do not celebrate the fire rate — you are firing every frame, because
            the range check runs every frame, and that is the next lesson's problem.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>What the finished feature does, from the player's seat:</strong></p>

    <ol>
        <li>
            Something appears at the <strong>end of the barrel</strong> — not at the turret's centre,
            and not at the world origin.
        </li>
        <li>
            It flies toward <strong>where I was standing when it was fired</strong>. If I keep
            moving, it does not follow me: it commits to the shot it took.
        </li>
        <li>
            It travels at <strong>the same speed</strong> whether I am against the turret or at the
            far edge of its range.
        </li>
        <li>
            It <strong>cleans itself up</strong> a few seconds later if it hits nothing.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural constraint (the only thing I will hand you):</strong> you are editing
        two files and neither knows the other's details. <code>TurretController.cs</code> decides
        <em>when</em> to fire and <em>where</em> the bullet starts; <code>Projectile.cs</code> owns
        everything after that — <em>which place it is heading for</em>, how fast, and when it dies.
        The one thing that crosses the boundary is a <code>Transform</code>: the turret already
        holds the player it noticed in <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>, and it hands that reference to
        the clone. If you find yourself writing the bullet's speed into the turret, stop — the
        turret should not know that number exists.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Two Files, One Shot</h3>
        <ol>
<li>The turret fires from its muzzle rather than from its centre. Which of your parts does that change, and what would a bullet spawned at the turret's centre look like to the player?</li>
<li>The bullet is handed the player's <code>Transform</code>, yet it does not follow you. Which line makes the shot a commitment rather than a chase — and what single change would turn this bullet into one that homes in?</li>
<li>To turn this into a shotgun that fires five bullets in a spread, which of the two files do you rewrite — and what does your answer prove about why the split is worth having?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Break a feature into parts small enough to look each one up on its own, and say where you reread the part you lifted from an earlier lesson</li>
        <li>Explain why the bullet starts at the muzzle, and why the turret hands it a <code>Transform</code> instead of computing a direction</li>
        <li>Point at the line that turns a live target into a snapshot, and say what the player would see if that read happened every frame</li>
        <li>Explain why <code>Vector3.MoveTowards()</code> needs no subtraction and no <code>.normalized</code>, and why the bullet needs no Rigidbody 2D</li>
        <li>Explain what a lifespan is protecting you from, and why your turret currently fires every frame</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/index.html">Unit 3: The Turret</a> — the projectile lifecycle and aiming in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — the clone call, the destroy call, and <code>Vector3.MoveTowards()</code></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/ask.html">How do I&hellip;? index</a> — find this lesson again by the question you were asking</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="3.3.html">3.3 — Fire Rate: Cooldowns &amp; Time</a>, where you fix the bullet hose you just built.</p>
</div>`;
